# ADR-0006 - Structural Markers for Performance Orientation

## Status

Proposed

## Context

MusicStewardship models performance structure at levels above individual recordings. A performance is not merely a sequence of tracks, but an intentional arrangement of performance objects including tandas, cortinas, sets, thematic sections, energy arcs, transitions, announcements, demonstrations, breaks, and other forms of content.

Many playback and playlist-management systems represent a performance as a flat list of playable items. While sufficient for playback, such representations often obscure the higher-level structure that guided the performance's design.

Experiments suggest that simple visual structural markers can make this structure visible without requiring specialized support from playback applications. These markers act as headings, landmarks, and navigational aids within an otherwise linear playlist.

## Structural Marker Grammar

A structural marker is a named object whose primary purpose is to communicate structure rather than content.

Structural markers form a lightweight visual grammar that allows higher-level performance concepts to be represented within systems that only understand linear sequences.

The grammar consists of:

* Visual delimiters
* Optional graphic symbols
* Structural vocabulary
* Human-readable names

Examples:

```text id="1sp24f"
=== TANDA: DI SARLI ===

=== CORTINA: SURF GUITAR ===

=== SET: GOLDEN AGE RHYTHMIC ===

=== BREAK ===
```

or:

```text id="5gqz9x"
▶ TANDA: DI SARLI

▶ CORTINA: SURF GUITAR

▶ SET: GOLDEN AGE RHYTHMIC
```

or:

```text id="mnsu54"
■ TANDA: DI SARLI

◆ CORTINA: SURF GUITAR

▲ SET: GOLDEN AGE RHYTHMIC
```

The exact visual form is less important than consistency and immediate recognizability.

